<keyword> 4.1

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.1   Source Code Comments                                   |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       A source code comment is a line of text or portion of a line of
       text that is ignored by the compiler.  Source code comments can
       be inserted almost anywhere within a program's source code by
       using the # character.  Some examples:

          <clr=49># this is a comment<clr=48>

          Var
            SomeVar Byte         <clr=49># this is another comment<clr=48>
          Begin
            <clr=49># another comment...<clr=48>
          End

       The # character is also used to include another .mps source
       file into program.  For more information on this, see the
       section on <a href=4.9>include</a> files.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.2

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.2   Defining Variables                                     |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       Defining variables can be done in the following format:

          VAR [variable name] [variable type]

       The following variable types are supported:

          Type            Format            Range
          --------- ------------ -----------------------------
          Boolean   FALSE/TRUE   0..1
          Char      Text         1 character
          String    Text         Sequence of 1..255 characters
          Byte      Numerical    0..255
          Integer   Numerical    -32767..32767
          Word      Numerical    0..65535
          LongInt   Numerical    -2147483648..214748364
          --------- ------------ -----------------------------

       Multiple variables can be defined with a single VAR command if
       a comma is added after the variable type:

          Var
            SomeByte Byte,
            SomeChar Char

       Single dimension Array variables can be defined by appending
       the ARRAY command after the variable type:

          Var
            SomeArray Byte Array(1..20)

       Constant variables can also be defined using the following
       format:

          CONST [variable name] = [value]

       The MPL compilers will automatically determine the variable
       type based on the value provided.  For example:

          Const
            MyStr  = 'This is a constant string'
            MyByte = 100

       As a general note, any variables defined within a procedure
       are accessible only by that procedure.  Any variables defined
       in the main program block are global to all procedures found
       after the definition:

          Var
            MyGlobalVar Byte

          Procedure Test
          Var
            Count Byte
          Begin
            WriteLn ('Global variable is ' + Int2Str(MyGlobalVar))
          Pend

          Begin
            WriteLn ('Global variable is ' + Int2Str(MyGlobalVar))
          End

       In the above code, the MyGlobalVar variable can be used in
       the Test procedure and throughout.  The Count variable can
       only be used within the Test procedure.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.3

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.3   Equation Operators                                     |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       MPL does not follow the order of operations when calculating
       mathmatical equations (ie PEMDAS - parenthesis, exponents,
       multiplication, division, addition, and subtraction).

       Equations are calculated in the order that they appear in the
       expression.  This doesn't mean that equations cannot be done
       by order of operations, it just means that they will have to
       be arranged differently in some cases.

       The following operation codes are supported for mathmatical
       equations:

          -   Subtraction
          +   Addition
          *   Multiplication
          /   Division
          MOD Modulus Operator

       Boolean operations are addressed using the keywords TRUE and
       FALSE.  When evaluating a Boolean value, the TRUE keyword can
       optionally be omitted.  For example:

          Var
            SomeBol Boolean

          Begin
            If SomeBol Then
              WriteLn ('SomeBol is TRUE!')
            EndIf
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.4

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.4   IF/THEN/ELSE Statements                                |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       The IF statement in MPL goes along with several other keywords
       for evaluating expressions: IF/THEN/ELSE/ELSEIF/ENDIF, which
       are used in the following format:

         IF [boolean statement] THEN
           code here
         ELSEIF [boolean statement] THEN
           code here
         ELSE
           code here
         ENDIF

       The ELSEIF and ELSE commands are optional.  The basic IF statment
       must consist of at least an IF and ENDIF command:

         If SomeVar = 10 Then
           WriteLn ('SomeVar is 10')
         EndIf

       Using ELSEIF and ELSE, a much more complicated branch can be
       evaluated:

         If SomeVar = 10 Then
           WriteLn ('SomeVar is 10')
         ElseIf SomeVar = 11 Then
           WriteLn ('SomeVar is 11')
         Else
           WriteLn ('SomeVar is not 10 or 11')
         EndIf

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.5

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.5   WHILE/WEND Statements                                  |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

        One method of creating a code loop is by using the WHILE and
        WEND keywords.  An alternative to this is the <a href=4.6>REPEAT/UNTIL</a>
        loop.  WHILE/WEND uses the following syntax:

           WHILE [boolean statement]
             code here
           WEND

        For example:

           Var
             SomeVar Byte
           Begin
             SomeVar := 0

             While SomeVar < 10
               SomeVar := SomeVar + 1
             Wend
           End

       The above example will repeat the code between the WHILE and
       WENT statements while the value of SomeVar is less than 10.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.6

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.6   REPEAT/UNTIL Statements                                |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

        One method of creating a code loop is by using the REPEAT and
        UNTIL keywords.  An alternative to this is the <a href=4.5>WHILE/WEND</a>
        loop.  REPEAT uses the following syntax:

           REPEAT
             code here
           UNTIL [boolean statement]

        For example:

           Var
             SomeVar Byte
           Begin
             SomeVar := 0

             Repeat
               SomeVar := SomeVar + 1
             Until SomeVar = 10
           End

       The above example will repeat the code between the REPEAT and
       UNTIL statements until the value of SomeVar equals 10.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.7

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.7   FOR/FEND Loops                                         |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       The FOR/FEND loop can be used to create a loop that cycles for
       and specified number of times before exiting.  The FOR/FEND
       loop uses the following syntax:

       FOR [variable] := [start value] [mode] [end value]
         code here
       FEND

       The [variable] must be a variable of a numeric type and holds
       the current count of the loop.  The [start value] is where the
       loop count will start, the [end value] is where the loop count
       will end.  The [mode] variable can be one of two keywords: TO
       or DOWNTO.  The TO keyword will cause the loop to increment
       each step while the DOWNTO keyword will cause the loop to
       decrement each step.

       Example:

         Var
           Count Byte

         Begin
           WriteLn ('Counting forwards...')

           For Count := 1 to 5
             WriteLn ('Loop #' + Int2Str(Count))
           Fend

           WriteLn ('Counting backwards...')

           For Count := 5 DownTo 1
             WriteLn ('Loop #' + Int2Str(Count))
           Fend
         End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.8

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.8   Defining Procedures And Functions                      |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       Procedures are a section of code that performs a specific
       action.  Procedures can also have a set of parameters passed
       to it, or return a result.  A procedure that returns a result
       is called a FUNCTION.  The Syntax for a defining a procedure
       is as follows:

          PROCEDURE [procedure name]

       or

          PROCEDURE [procedure name] (parameters)

       Example:

          Procedure Test
          Begin
            WriteLn ('This is a test')
          Pend

          Procedure PrintString (Str String)
          Begin
            WriteLn (Str)
          Pend

          Begin
            Test
            PrintString('Hello world!')
          End

       The above example defines two procedures, one without any
       data passed to it, and one with data being passed to it.  When
       the program is executed, the Procedures are not executed until
       called within the main program block.  When a procedure is
       finished execution will return to the spot where the procedure
       was called.

       Procedures with a result value are called FUNCTIONS.  These use
       the same sytax as a procedure except they use the keyword
       FUNCTION and declare a result variable type:

          FUNCTION [function name] ( parameters ) : [result type]

       For example:

          Function MakeFalse (Bol Boolean) : Boolean
          Begin
            If Bol Then
              Bol := False
            EndIf

            MakeFile := Bol
          Pend

          Var MyBoolean Boolean

          Begin
            MyBoolean := True
            MyBoolean := MakeFalse(MyBoolean)
          End

       The above example defines a Function called MakeFalse.  This
       takes a boolean parameter, makes it false and returns the
       value.  Functions are used in expressions and evaluations
       since they have a resulting value, thus in this example
       MyBoolean will be set to False since the result of MakeFalse
       is stored in MyBoolean.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 4.9

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 4.9   Using Include Files                                    |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       Source files can be included within other source files using
       a variation of the comment system.  This can be useful for
       creating a library of common procedures or to split up a large
       projects into more organized smaller files.  The syntax for
       include files is as follows:

          #include [filename]

       Example:

          #include common.mps

          Begin
          End

       In the above example, any variables and procedures found within
       the common.mps file will be included into the current source tree
       as if it were all a single file.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

