<keyword> CMD PATHCHAR

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>PATHCHAR                           <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function PathChar : Char

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This function returns either a foreslash or backslash based
          on the type of file system.  For example, when an MPE program
          is executed in Linux, PATHCHAR will return "/".  When that same
          MPE is executed under DOS, Windows, or OS/2, a "\" will be
          returned.

          This function is needed when doing an file I/O if you want your
          programs to be portable between all versions of Mystic BBS.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Var
            FileName String

          Begin
            FileName := 'TEMP' + Int2Str(NodeNum) + PathChar + 'TEST.TXT')

            fOpen    (1, Text, ReWrite, FileName)
            fWriteLn (1, 'This is a test!')
            fClose   (1)

            DispFile(FileName)
          End

       The above example uses PATHCHAR in place of where a backslash
       would have been.  This will allow the file 'TEST.TXT' to be
       created in Unix-type file systems AND Dos-type file systems.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 5.3

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 5.3   Date and Time Functions                                |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

            <a href=CMD DATESTR> DATESTR </a>   - Converts a date/time stamp to a string date
            <a href=CMD DATETIME> DATETIME </a>  - Returns the current date/time stamp
            <a href=CMD TIMER> TIMER </a>     - Returns the number of seconds since midnight
            <a href=CMD TIMESTR> TIMESTR </a>   - Converts a date/time stamp to a string time

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD DATESTR

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>DATESTR                            <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function DATESTR (DT LongInt, Format Byte) : String

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This function will take a packed datetime number and convert it to
          a date string.  The dType parameter is used to set the format of the
          date which is returned.  Valid types are:

              0 = Returns date in format that the user has set.
              1 = Returns date in format: MM/DD/YY
              2 = Returns date in format: DD/MM/YY
              3 = Returns date in format: YY/DD/MM

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          GetThisUser
          WriteLn ('Welcome.  You last called on ' + DateStr(UserLast))

       The above example loads the currently logged in user's information
       into the USER variables using GETTHISUSER, then displays the last
       time the user has called to the screen using the DATESTR procedure.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD DATETIME

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>DATETIME                           <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

         Function DATETIME : LongInt

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

         This function returns the current Date and Time in the packed
         format.  DATESTR and TIMESTR can be used to convert this value
         into strings.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

         WriteLn ('Current Date: ' + DateStr(DateTime, 0))
         WriteLn ('Current Time: ' + TimeStr(DateTime, 1))<clr=48>

       The above example outputs the current date and time to the screen
       using the DATESTR and TIMESTR functions.  The DateTime function
       which is passed to DATESTR and TIMESTR, contains the current date
       and time in the packed format.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD TIMER

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>TIMER                              <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function TIMER : LongInt

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This function returns the number of seconds which have passed
          since midnight.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          WriteLn ('Seconds since midnight: ' + Int2Str(Timer))

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD TIMESTR

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>TIMESTR                            <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function TIMESTR (DT LongInt, Format Byte) : String

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This function takes a packed date/time record and returns the
          time in a string format, where Format is one of the following:

             0 = Returns time string in 24 format: 17:32
             1 = Returns time string in 12 format: 10:21a

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          GetThisUser
          WriteLn ('Last call on: ' + DateStr(UserLast, 0))
          WriteLn ('          at: ' + TimeStr(UserLast, 1))

       The above example uses GetThisUser to read information on the
       current user.  It then uses DateSTR and TimeSTR to display the
       last time and date that the user called.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 5.4

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 5.4   BBS Data Access Functions                              |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

            <a href=CMD GETCFG> GETCFG </a>       - Reads current configuration data
            <a href=CMD GETFBASE> GETFBASE </a>     - Reads file base configuration data
            <a href=CMD GETFGROUP> GETFGROUP </a>    - Reads file group configuation data
            <a href=CMD GETMBASE> GETMBASE </a>     - Reads message base configuration data
            <a href=CMD GETMGROUP> GETMGROUP </a>    - Reads message group configuration data
            <a href=CMD GETPROMPT> GETPROMPT </a>    - Reads a prompt from the current language
            <a href=CMD GETSCREENINFO> GETSCREENINFO </a>- Reads values of ScreenInfo MCI codes
            <a href=CMD GETTEXTSTR> GETTEXTSTR </a>   - Reads data from the text buffer
            <a href=CMD GETTHISUSER> GETTHISUSER </a>  - Reads data from the current user
            <a href=CMD GETUSER> GETUSER </a>      - Reads data from the user database
            <a href=CMD PUTFBASE> PUTFBASE </a>     - Writes file base configuration data
            <a href=CMD PUTFGROUP> PUTFGROUP </a>    - Writes file group configuration data
            <a href=CMD PUTMBASE> PUTMBASE </a>     - Writes message base configuration data
            <a href=CMD PUTMGROUP> PUTMGROUP </a>    - Writes message group configuration data
            <a href=CMD PUTTEXTSTR> PUTTEXTSTR </a>   - Writes data from the text buffer
            <a href=CMD PUTTHISUSER> PUTTHISUSER </a>  - Writes data from the current user
            <a href=CMD PUTUSER> PUTUSER </a>      - Writes data to the user database
            <a href=CMD SETPROMPT> SETPROMPT </a>    - Writes a prompt to the current language
            <a href=CMD USES> USES </a>         - Defines support for BBS data modules

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD USES

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>USES                               <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Uses [module type]

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          The USES command declares that a certain "group" of predefined
          MPE variables and functions will be used in your program.  It
          should be declared at the top of your program, before any other
          code, and must contain one of the valid modules:

             CFG    - Allow use of Configuration variables and commands
             DIR    - Allow use of Directory reading variables and commands
             FBASE  - Allow use of File Base variables and commands
             FGROUP - Allow use of File Group variables and commands
             MBASE  - Allow use of Message Base variables and commands
             MGROUP - Allow use of Message Group variables and commands
             USER   - Allow use of User Base variables and commands

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Uses USER

          Begin
            GetThisUser

            WriteLn ('Welcome to this program ' + UserHandle)
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 5.5

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 5.5   Input Functions                                        |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

            <a href=CMD INPUT> INPUT </a>      - Prompts the user for input
            <a href=CMD INPUTNY> INPUTNY </a>    - Prompts user with a Yes/No question
            <a href=CMD INPUTYN> INPUTYN </a>    - Prompts user with a Yes/No question
            <a href=CMD ISARROW> ISARROW </a>    - TRUE if last key was an arrow key
            <a href=CMD ISLOCALKEY> ISLOCALKEY </a> - TRUE if last key was from local keyboard
            <a href=CMD KEYPRESSED> KEYPRESSED </a> - TRUE is a key is in the input buffer
            <a href=CMD ONEKEY> ONEKEY </a>     - Reads a single character from the keyboard
            <a href=CMD READKEY> READKEY </a>    - Reads a single character from the keyboard
            <a href=CMD STUFFKEY> STUFFKEY </a>   - Stuffs characters into the input buffer

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD INPUTNY

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>INPUTNY                            <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function InputNY (Text String) : Boolean

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          Prompts the user with a Yes/No question, defaulting the input to
          NO.  The function will return TRUE if the user selects Yes, and
          FALSE if the user selects NO.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          If Not InputNY('Do you want to run this program?') Then
            Halt
          EndIf

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD INPUTYN

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>INPUTYN                            <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function InputYN (Text String) : Boolean

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          Prompts the user with a Yes/No question, defaulting the input to
          YES.  The function will return TRUE if the user selects Yes, and
          FALSE if the user selects NO.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          If Not InputYN('Do you want to run this program?') Then
            Halt
          EndIf

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD ISARROW

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>ISARROW                            <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function IsArrow : Boolean

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

         IsArrow is used along with the ReadKey function to process arrow keys
         and other types of extended input.  It should be called directly
         after calling ReadKey and returns TRUE if the character returned is
         an extended input character.

         Extended input characters are:

         ASCII #     Chr     Key Pressed
         -------     ---     -----------
           71         G      Home
           72         H      Up Arrow
           73         I      Page Up
           75         K      Left Arrow
           77         M      Right Arrow
           79         O      End
           80         P      Down Arrow
           81         Q      Page Down
           83         S      Delete

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

         Var Ch Char

         Ch := ReadKey

         If IsArrow Then
           If Ch = Chr(72) Then
             WriteLn ('Up arrow has been entered')
           ElseIf Ch = Chr(80) Then
             WriteLn ('Down arrow has been entered')
           EndIf
         Else
           WriteLn ('Regular key received: ' + Ch)
         EndIf

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD ISLOCALKEY

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>ISLOCALKEY                         <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function IsLocalKey : Boolean

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          IsLocalKey is used along with the READKEY function to determine if
          the character just received was entered from the local or remote
          keyboard.  TRUE will be returned if the key was entered from the
          local keyboard.  FALSE will be returned if the key was entered
          from a remote keyboard.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Ch := ReadKey

          If IsLocalKey Then
            WriteLn ('Key pressed from the local keyboard.')
          Else
            WriteLn ('Key pressed from the remote keyboard.')
          EndIf

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> 5.6

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      | 5.6   Output Functions                                       |
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

            <a href=CMD CLS> CLS </a>          - Clears the screen
            <a href=CMD DISPFILE> DISPFILE </a>     - Displays a file to the user
            <a href=CMD GOTOXY> GOTOXY </a>       - Moves the cursor to a X/Y position
            <a href=CMD ISNOFILE> ISNOFILE </a>     - TRUE after DISPFILE if file doesn't exist
            <a href=CMD MOVEX> MOVEX </a>        - Moves the cursor to a X coordinate
            <a href=CMD MOVEY> MOVEY </a>        - Moves the cursor to a Y coordinate
            <a href=CMD TEXTATTR> TEXTATTR </a>     - Returns the current text attribute
            <a href=CMD TEXTCOLOR> TEXTCOLOR </a>    - Changes the current text color
            <a href=CMD WHEREX> WHEREX </a>       - Returns the current X cursor position
            <a href=CMD WHEREY> WHEREY </a>       - Returns the current Y cursor position
            <a href=CMD WRITE> WRITE </a>        - Writes a string/text to the screen
            <a href=CMD WRITELN> WRITELN </a>      - Writes a string/text to the screen with a CR
            <a href=CMD WRITELOCALXY> WRITELOCALXY </a> - Writes text to the local screen only

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD CLS

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>CLS                                <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure CLS

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          Clears the screen and returns the cursor to the top left side of
          the screen.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          CLS
          WriteLn ('The screen was just cleared!')

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD DISPFILE

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>DISPFILE                           <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure DISPFILE (FileName String)

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This procedure displays a text or ANSI file to the screen.  If a
          path not included in the passed filename, Mystic will look for
          the file in the language text file directory.  If no file
          extension is provided in the passed file name, Mystic will display
          the correct file according to the user's graphics settings
          (ie .ANS for ANSI, .ASC for non-ansi).

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          DispFile ('WELCOME')

       The above example will display the text file "WELCOME" from the
       language text file directory.  Since there is no file extension
       provided, Mystic will display "WELCOME.ANS" or "WELCOME.ASC"
       depending on what the user's terminal settings are.

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD GOTOXY

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>GOTOXY                             <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure GotoXY (X Byte, Y Byte)

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          Locates the cursor to the specified X and Y position on the screen.
          This procedure only works if the user has ANSI terminal emulation
          enabled.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          CLS
          GotoXY (1, 10)
          Write ('This is the 10th line of the screen')<clr=48>

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD ISNOFILE

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>ISNOFILE                           <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function IsNoFile : Boolean

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          Returns TRUE after a call to DISPFILE if the file that was
          attemped to display did not exist.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Begin
            DispFile('HELLO')

            If IsNoFile Then
              WriteLn ('HELLO.XXX does not exist.')
            EndIf
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD MOVEX

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>MOVEX                              <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure MoveX (XPos Byte)

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This procedure moves the cursor from the current position to
          the passed X coordinate on the screen.  This procedure only
          works if the user has ANSI compatible terminal.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Begin
            MoveX (25)
            Write ('This text is on column 25')
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD MOVEY

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>MOVEY                              <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure MoveY (YPos Byte)

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This procedure moves the cursor from the current position to
          the passed Y coordinate on the screen.  This procedure only
          works if the user has ANSI compatible terminal.
          
       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Begin
            MoveY (10)
            Write ('This text is on line 10')
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD TEXTATTR

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>TEXTATTR                           <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Function TextAttr : Byte

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This function returns the current text attribute.  See
          the help for <a href=CMD WRITELOCALXY> WRITELOCALXY </a> for a detailed
          description of the text attribute colors.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Begin
            WriteLn ('The current text attribute is: ' + Int2Str(TextAttr))
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

<keyword> CMD TEXTCOLOR

      .--------------------------------------------------------------.
      |                           <clr=52>TEXTCOLOR                          <clr=48>|
      `--------------------------------------------------------------'

       <clr=49>Syntax:<clr=48>

          Procedure TextColor (Color Byte)

       <clr=49>Description:<clr=48>

          This procedure changes the current text color to the passed
          text attribute.  See the help for <a href=CMD WRITELOCALXY> WRITELOCALXY </a>
          for more detailed information on the text attribute.

       <clr=49>Example:<clr=48>

          Begin
            TextColor (31)
            WriteLn ('This text is a White on Blue Background!')
          End

       --------------------------------------------------------------
       <a href=PREVIOUS>Previous Topic</a>                                 <a href=INDEX>Return to Index</a>
<end>

